            <ArticleTitle>Diagnostic value of the recovery time-course of st slope on exercise ECG in discriminating false-from true-positive ST-segment depressions.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>915-22</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Using the exercise ECG for diagnosing coronary artery disease (CAD) is hampered by the occurrence of false-positive (FP) ST-segment depression. Because it is known that the recovery ST-T time-course in CAD differs from that in FP subjects, the ST slope may help discriminate FP from true-positive (TP) results.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS AND RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Treadmill digitized ECG from patients with significant ST-segment depressions and normal resting ECG were analyzed in 134 patients with CAD on angiography (&gt;50% narrowing) and reversible perfusion defects (TP group), and 64 subjects with normal perfusion (FP group) on exercise single photon emission computed tomography. The ST slope between the J-point and J(80) was measured every minute up to 6-min postexercise. The ST slope was significantly higher in FP than in TP at peak exercise, and at postexercise 1-, 2- and 3-min (p&lt;0.01, all). Thereafter, it gradually increased in TP, while monotonically decreasing in FP. Its decrease from 3- to 6-min could correctly diagnose 88% of FP subjects, whereas it was found in only 19% of TP patients (total accuracy 83%).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The ST slope change from early to late recovery is a simple yet reliable marker for discriminating FP from TP ST-segment responses in subjects with a normal resting ECG.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
